Algorithm: Merge Two Sorted Arrays
Step 1: Initialize three pointers:
        One at the end of the valid elements in the first array.
        One at the end of the second array.
        One at the last position of the first array (total capacity).
Step 2: Compare the elements pointed to in both arrays starting from the end.
Step 3: Place the larger element at the last available position of the first array.
Step 4: Move the pointer of the array from which the element was taken and also move the position pointer backward.
Step 5: Repeat this process until one of the arrays is completely traversed.
Step 6: If elements still remain in the second array, copy them into the remaining positions of the first array.
Step 7: The first array will now contain all elements in sorted order.

Time Complexity: O(m + n)
Space Complexity: O(1)

-----> Code <-----

class Solution {
    public void merge(int[] nums1, int m, int[] nums2, int n) {
        int k=m+n-1;
        m=m-1;
        n=n-1;
        while(m>=0 && n>=0 ){
            if(nums1[m]<nums2[n]){
                nums1[k]=nums2[n];
                k--;
                n--;
            }else{
                nums1[k]=nums1[m];
                m--;
                k--;
            }
        }
        while(n>=0){
            nums1[k]=nums2[n];
            k--;
            n--;
        }
    }
}
